Material from other projects
============================

1. The Linux kernel's v3d driver (drivers/gpu/drm/v3d, Raspberry Pi kernel
   rpi-6.12.y): GPL-2.0-or-later, Copyright (C) 2014-2018 Broadcom,
   Copyright (C) 2023 Raspberry Pi.
   - core/v3d_regs.h is Linux's file, unchanged.
   - core/v3d_hw.c, core/v3d_sched.c, core/v3d_bo.c and core/v3d_ioctl.c
     follow Linux's v3d_drv.c, v3d_gem.c, v3d_mmu.c, v3d_irq.c,
     v3d_sched.c, v3d_bo.c and v3d_submit.c.

2. Linux's v3d request interface (include/uapi/drm/v3d_drm.h): MIT licence,
   Copyright (C) 2014-2018 Broadcom. include/drm/v3d_drm.h is that file,
   unchanged, with its notice, as Mesa carries it (include/drm-uapi,
   with the reset counters of newer kernels).

3. Rob Heaton's V3DDriver (github.com/robheaton/riscos-v3d): GPL-2.0-or-
   later, Copyright (C) 2026 Rob Heaton. riscos/plat_pi4.c follows its
   Pi 4 power-up (v3d_power_on), its check for a V3D that is already
   running (v3d_hw_is_up), its bus-gate stop (v3d_stop_bridge), its
   firmware clock calls (v3d_clock.c) and its interrupt device number
   (v3d_irq.c). V3DDriver took the register offsets and the power-on
   sequence from rpi4-gpu-bare-metal-examples (MIT licence, Copyright (c)
   2022 Random06457, github.com/Random06457/rpi4-gpu-bare-metal-examples),
   whose notice follows.

